  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Friday)\nIssued at 1043 AM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nCurrent radar depicts snow showers continuing to move east across\nthe western South Dakota plains. Heavier snow bands are reducing\nvisibility to less than 3 miles. Snow will move out of the area \nby later this evening. As of this forecast, areas in the northern\nBlack Hills and far northeast Wyoming have gotten a few inches, \nwith snow depth tapering off farther eastward. Satellite imagery \nshows a closed upper low over southern Saskatchewan, which looks \nto swing down through northern MN into the Great Lakes region. \nAnother large closed upper low is spinning off the Pac northwest \ncoast. Temperatures tonight will cool down to between 20 and 10 \ndegrees for most of the region. Some patchy fog is also possible \nin some areas of the Black Hills early Sunday morning.\n\nHeight rises will usher the aforementioned Canadian upper low out\nof the area over the rest of the weekend. That in tandem with \nsurface pressure rises will bring warm temperatures to start next \nweek. An area of high pressure will spill out of Canada down into \nthe Great Plains, following yesterday's cold front. Warm \ntemperatures up to seasonal normals are expected by Wednesday, and\nabove normal through the rest of the week. \n\nLooking ahead, cool front will move through the area on \nWednesday. No precipitation or strong winds are expected to come\nwith it. Thanksgiving weather looks to be pleasant with area-wide\nhighs hovering a few degrees around 50. By the end of next week,\nthe chaotic upper pattern will move out and large scale ridging\nwill dominate the western US. Northwest flow will reside over the\nnorthern Great Plains. No meaningful precipitation is expected\nthrough the next 7 days.",
